WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.710
- What I want to talk about
is work I've done in...

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.230
Yeah, inferring nature,
in physics, biology,

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.240
and using machine learning.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.680
And to keep things interesting,

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.390
I'm applying to phrase the conversation

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.370
as if it's a match
between humans and nature,

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.570
where humans score points

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.880
when they infer something new about nature

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.150
and nature scores points when
it's making it harder for us

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.373
and trying to to keep us at bay.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.140
So yeah, so you might think
that since everybody here,

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.040
so I'm talking to people,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.570
some might think that be
we would be team human,

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.330
but even ancient Greece where
the skeptics that maintain

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.120
that nature cannot be known.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.970
So maybe even in the
audience there's like this...

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.053
Like different teams.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.960
Excellent, so we started with
this fundamental question,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.820
what are the fundamental laws?

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.983
The fundamental axioms of the universe?

00:00:56.853 --> 00:00:58.798
And the thing is that we don't know.

00:00:58.798 --> 00:01:01.320
We don't know and this is depressing

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.360
because we start being very ambitious,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.120
and trying to kick off the project

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.280
of inferring nature off the ground.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.770
But there is no ground,
there is no bedrock

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.480
upon which to build systematically

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.480
and trying to infer the rest of nature.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.670
This is in stark contrast with mathematics

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.430
where we have axioms that we postulate,

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.530
and then we take for granted as bedrock

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.450
and we start inferring.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.890
Alex was very nicely explaining

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.370
how there are some prerequisites
for picking those axioms,

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.250
but then we use them as given,

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.630
whereas we don't have that in science.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.460
There's no bedrock, it's more like a swamp

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.860
that you're trying to
bootstrap yourself out of.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.200
So why do I say that we don't have

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.543
the fundamental axioms of the universe?

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.510
It's because experiments suggest

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.980
that there are four forces

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.710
that describe interaction
of matter with other matter,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.570
electromagnetism, the
strong nuclear force,

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.030
the weak nuclear force, and gravity.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.990
And the first three have been unified

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.700
in a common framework that we call

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.740
the standard model of particle physics.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.580
But the fourth one
hasn't been incorporated

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.630
into a common framework.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.720
This is why we don't have
the axioms because we cannot,

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.920
in a unified framework,
describe everything.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.400
And the reason for that is ultimately

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.900
how they deal with information.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.650
The first three forces
explicitly preserve information,

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.510
whereas the fourth force,

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.060
at least on face value,
seems to destroy it.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.100
And since you can't have
both simultaneously,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.900
it's very hard to put them together.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.890
And it's very hard to put them together

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.530
but it's not like string
theorists have given up

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.990
and they have been trying to resolve

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.870
this information paradox
first introduced by Hawking,

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:42.903
coincidentally was in
previous PhD committee.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.760
And what they have figured out lately,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.530
as of five years ago,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.360
is that information seems
to be lost in our universe

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.550
because of the presence of baby universes

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.617
that constantly split off of our universe

00:02:58.617 --> 00:03:00.150
and then merge back onto it.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.800
And if you take into account those effects

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.960
of universes splitting off of ours

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.550
and merging to this one,

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.050
you can actually reproduce this effect

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.640
that information seems to be lost

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.700
because you only have
access to one universe.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.140
And there was a paper, a similar paper

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.840
by Maxfield and Marolf in 2020

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.840
and also our paper from last year

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.950
that, yeah, reproduces this effect.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.794
So in our paper we used
topological quantum gravity,

00:03:25.794 --> 00:03:29.340
so we had the regular gravity
you have in our universe

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.860
and we unified it and reproduced,

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.660
resolved Hawking's Paradox.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.390
Not with these three forces
that I'm showing there

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.210
but with another force which was described

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.440
by topological quantum
field theory so we did...

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.630
So in this model, which is rigorous

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.770
and analytically exactly computable model,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.410
you had a common framework
where we accept gravity

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.300
and non-gravitational forces,

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.600
and you are resolving
the information paradox.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.620
But so there is a lot of
progress I'm trying to say,

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.870
but still we unify gravity

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.780
with a completely random other force,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.790
not with these three so still,

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.370
we don't have the fundamental axioms.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.322
And you might say that, "Oh, that's bad."

00:04:14.322 --> 00:04:17.610
But now I'm doing computational biology,

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:19.650
so we don't need to...

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:20.970
We don't need gravity for that.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.950
And if I'm saying that we understand

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.250
the first three forces nearly perfectly,

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.983
then why not make a simulation?

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:33.430
Oh, by the way, yeah, so
this lack of laws of physics

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.680
is how nature keeps us at bay
and poses the first challenge,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.083
nobody gives us the
axioms of of the universe.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.750
But like I was saying,
we don't need gravity

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.580
so how about doing the following thing

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.410
like discretizing space

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:48.570
and putting the standard
model of particle physics

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.040
on that discretized space

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.499
and then try to perfectly simulate a cell.

00:04:52.499 --> 00:04:55.590
That would be super cool
because then we would be able

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:58.533
to find initial particle
configuration in the cell,

00:04:58.533 --> 00:05:01.560
then simulate a drug coming in

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.510
and seeing exactly what effect would have.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.840
So we'd be able to have initial
calculate drugs and stuff.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:08.820
So can we perfectly simulate a cell?

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.800
And of course there's nothing
particular about the cell.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.240
Can we simulate the universe
or parts of it perfectly?

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.250
And unfortunately there's a theorem

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.680
that says that you cannot do that.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.600
It's the Nielsen-Ninomiya theorem

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.060
and more precisely it says,

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.570
that you cannot simulate
the standard portal...

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:29.956
The standard model of particle physics

00:05:29.956 --> 00:05:33.213
in on a discretized space.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:36.480
And since the standard
model of particle physics

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.650
is our universe that means

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.170
that you can't simulate the universe.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.780
The proof of that relies in two ideas.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:44.613
The idea is...

00:05:44.613 --> 00:05:45.660
The first idea is that the standard model

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.270
has chiral particles, which means that

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:51.570
when the particle
rotates, spins like this,

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:54.090
it can move in only one of two directions,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.000
either this way or or the opposite way.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.600
And you call this right-handed
and this left-handed.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.910
Yeah, so those are the
only two possibilities

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:03.743
that it allows.

00:06:03.743 --> 00:06:05.100
And in fact, it's
interesting that it picks...

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:05.970
Prefers the right-hand...

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.280
The left-handed particles

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.290
but tho those are the
only two possibilities.

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:11.661
And the second idea is something

00:06:11.661 --> 00:06:14.040
that we learn in undergrad physics,

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:18.003
that when you have discretized
space, momentum is periodic.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.180
That means that identical...

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.040
Like that values of the momentum

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.740
periodically are completely equivalent.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.200
Using those two ideas, you can prove

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.850
that the standard model
of particle physics

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:31.560
cannot exist in discretized space.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:32.850
Because if you have a particle,

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.040
right-hand particle like that,

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.440
then this positive momentum

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.030
since we're in discretized space,

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:42.630
would be completely
equivalent to being negative.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:43.800
But that means that this particle

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.960
will be both left-handed and right-handed.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.940
But the so there molecule part...

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.420
So the model that treats
those particles differently,

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.030
which is an absurd and
(indistinct) absurd,

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.573
and we prove that this
cannot be happening.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:03.150
So, there's another way to look at this.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.710
So this answers the question

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:05.970
of whether we live in the matrix

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:09.900
as Professor David Tong
from Trinity College

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:10.890
likes to phrase it.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.180
If we cannot simulate the universe,

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:15.574
then this indeed could
not be a simulation.

00:07:15.574 --> 00:07:17.790
We cannot be living in the matrix

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:20.440
because then CERN wouldn't be giving

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.523
the data results that it is giving us.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:28.320
Yeah, however, it's nice to say that...

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.320
It's comforting that

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:35.220
what I'm experiencing is not a simulation

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.040
but from a scientific point
of view it's depressing.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:39.120
It's another point for nature

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.490
because we cannot have perfect simulations

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:44.673
of the fundamental laws
as we understand them.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:48.960
Oops...

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:49.830
Oops...

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:53.370
Oh, yeah so we don't know
the axioms of the universe.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:54.810
Even when we do know them,

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:57.003
we cannot perfectly simulate them.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.520
What's left then?

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.400
Well clearly, what's
left is to do experiments

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.946
but they come with their own issues.

00:08:04.946 --> 00:08:09.240
And in particular all of analyzing data

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:10.350
because the moment you get your data,

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:11.880
you have to analyze
them and interpret them,

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:15.180
and then you run into the
generalizability problem,

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:19.170
which of course is like a cylinder.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.580
If you look at it from the top,

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:21.690
it manifests in different ways

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:25.467
depending on your perspective
and yeah, even in philosophy,

00:08:25.467 --> 00:08:27.660
people have looked into that, right?

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.550
Even in ancient Greece, Sextus Empiricus,

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.815
David Hume that we heard about.

00:08:31.815 --> 00:08:33.720
Also the rule following,
I like to think of

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:35.370
as this Generalizability Problem.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:38.820
And yeah, so it consists of

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.050
the following thought experiment.

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:43.304
If you like have an apple and you drop it,

00:08:43.304 --> 00:08:45.690
you see it fall to the ground.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:48.330
And if you report that a
hundred times it keeps happening

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.329
but are you allowed the 101st time

00:08:51.329 --> 00:08:56.329
to conclude that it surely
will be like the previous ones

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.880
that it'll fall to the ground?

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.100
In other words, generalizability
is about arguing

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.340
from observed cases,
based on observed cases,

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.083
about unseen cases.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.130
And in machine learning we're
interested in those cases

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.380
when the unseen cases, we know are priori

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.280
that they're bound to different a bit.

00:09:18.431 --> 00:09:20.550
And yeah, so that's why I call it

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:22.290
Out Of Distribution prediction

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:25.590
or as of late, in the context
of foundational models,

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:27.663
we refer to it as zero-shot learning.

00:09:31.368 --> 00:09:35.070
Yeah, so and in that
context so in general,

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.560
if we know if we allow the unseen cases

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:40.740
to be arbitrarily different
than the seen cases,

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:44.430
then clearly we cannot possibly conclude

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:45.263
something about them.

00:09:45.263 --> 00:09:46.290
We can not conclude something

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:47.700
about what you haven't observed.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:48.630
And this is the...

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:50.520
This goes by the name essentially

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.803
of the no free lunch theorem.

00:09:54.025 --> 00:09:59.025
What it says more specifically
is that an algorithm

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.720
can only be better at finding
a data generating distribution

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:07.920
only at the expense of being
worse at finding another one,

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:09.420
at inferring another one.

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:13.080
So alternative algorithm
A will on average,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:16.050
have the same performance
than any other algorithm

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:19.083
when you average above
across all problems.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:23.300
Yeah, so this is the final...

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:26.850
Another point for nature,

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:30.180
which is leading by now by three points.

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:32.370
So how are we pushing back against

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:33.420
this generalizability problem?

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:37.050
What can we do about this issue?

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:38.641
Well, what we can do about it is...

00:10:38.641 --> 00:10:41.250
What comes to the rescue
is using constraints

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.680
from prior knowledge,
that's the way to get off.

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:44.880
So what general...

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.740
What the no free lunch theorem introduces

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:49.830
is this trade off between
bias and variants.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.080
The way to get rid of it,

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.900
is to introduce prior
knowledge constraints.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.120
And the way to do that is to
start gathering a lot of data

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:58.410
and start noticing patterns.

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:01.140
For instance, you can impose the pattern

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.330
that your model has to have sparsity

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:06.240
and therefore datasets
that could not be produced

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.120
by sparse models would be rejected.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.730
And that would allow your algorithm A,

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:15.960
to over to over perform other algorithms

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:18.410
in the relevant cases that
haven't been excluded.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.730
But of course in biology,

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:23.850
we're trying to push
further ahead from that

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:27.030
and that's what the Human
Cell Atlas tries to do.

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:28.680
instead of just using sparsity.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:30.420
We're introducing, we're compiling

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:31.800
very comprehensive data sets

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:36.090
of both observational
and interventional data

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:39.079
across many organs diseases, donors,

00:11:39.079 --> 00:11:43.050
both for single cell and also spatially.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:44.220
I'm not sure if this has a pointer

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:48.240
but like here you can see
that now we know that...

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.160
What genes are expressed in this point

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:52.445
versus what other genes are expressed

00:11:52.445 --> 00:11:54.503
in this other point of
this section of the tissue.

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.360
I should also say that
the Human Cell Atlas

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.360
is Co-Led by Professor Sarah
Teichmann and Aviv Regev.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:06.540
And it's this effort of trying to map

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:11.250
all the different cell types
that exist in the human body

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.410
because although all the cells in my body

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:18.270
have the same exactly genome,
they make different choices.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.970
And the cell in my eye
decides to be an eye cell

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:23.103
and the cell on my skin, a skin cell.

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:26.940
So they decide to focus on
different parts of the genome

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:30.600
and undergo what we refer
to as differentiation.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:34.890
And in order to understand
better human health and disease,

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:37.470
we're trying to map out all
the different possibilities

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:39.933
that cells have to opt for.

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:43.530
Yeah, so that's how we push back.

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:45.990
We start gathering now
biological prior knowledge

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:47.710
and build models based on that

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:51.540
and that's how we start
pushing back against nature

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:55.380
by compiling though this
is a very big consortium

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:58.503
and a huge effort in single cell biology.

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:04.500
And furthermore, in my work in progress

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.690
in the Lotfollahi Lab,

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:08.684
we're building this Dis2p model,

00:13:08.684 --> 00:13:11.520
which stands for disentangle covariates

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:12.933
to predict counterfactuals.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:16.380
And it lies at the intersection

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:20.067
between disentangled learning,
fairness, and causality.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:25.080
And our DA is that during
the training process,

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:30.080
we augment the data by matching
that up with one another

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:32.430
to produce counterfactual pairs.

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:34.230
And then during the training process,

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:37.200
we constantly do the three steps

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.360
of abduction, action, and prediction,

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:42.270
and penalize the training process.

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:44.010
So during the training process,

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:45.300
we're trying to predict counterfactuals

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.450
and make a model that
predicts them better.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:51.988
Yeah, in order to have
a better performance

00:13:51.988 --> 00:13:54.243
for out of distribution prediction.

00:13:57.390 --> 00:13:58.680
Another thing, what comes to the rescue

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:02.550
about not knowing the fundamental
axioms of the universe

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:05.160
is another idea from theoretical physics,

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:07.890
which is the renormalization group.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:11.010
And which essentially says that phenomena

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:15.330
at different length scales
are siloed from one another.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:19.290
They don't get...

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:22.050
So phenomena at the subatomic level

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:26.490
are not relevant for phenomena
at much larger length scales.

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:29.140
Yeah, more precisely what
it says is that a theory

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.670
that tries to explain a phenomena
at a specific length scale

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:36.760
should be phrased with degrees of freedom

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:40.530
that are in the same length scale.

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:44.400
And in that way, that
theory will manage...

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:49.400
We will manage to have it
be elegant and have it be...

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:54.000
Need a finite number of measurements

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:55.250
in order to be predicted.

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:59.730
Yeah, so imagine like in the
context of a disease spreading

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:01.560
clearly the relevant degrees
of freedom to capture

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:05.310
would be the bacteria and the where...

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:07.503
Like discovered by Robert Koch.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.080
And then we would be able
to build a nice theory

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.030
of how disease spreads.

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:14.850
Clearly we could also try to do it

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:16.170
with subatomic degrees of freedom

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:20.310
but we would put a huge
monster that could do it

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.870
but it wouldn't be any better in doing so

00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:25.740
than if we had used the
right degrees of freedom.

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:27.870
Because ultimately our immense success

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:30.150
in designing drugs and
understanding biology,

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:31.470
tells us that the tiny details

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:34.650
in which gravity is quantized,

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:37.350
in this way or in another
slightly different way,

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:39.120
couldn't possibly matter.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:41.730
That's what this theorem,

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:43.953
The Renormalization Group tells us.

00:15:45.355 --> 00:15:47.460
At the other extreme, you could try...

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:50.730
Think of trying to explain how
disease spreads by using...

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:54.420
Not allowing yourself to
use the notion of bacteria

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:59.420
and build a theory solely
at the people's level.

00:15:59.455 --> 00:16:02.130
And you don't have to
think to imagine that

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:03.270
because in medieval Europe

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:04.410
that's exactly what people were doing.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.480
They were using this now debunked theory

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:07.530
about how diseases spread,

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.650
the miasma theory invented
by ancient Greek physicians.

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:12.930
Miasma in Greek means a pollutant.

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.820
They were thinking of it as a cloud

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:17.190
that settles upon the town
and causes people to suffer,

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:19.110
and then that cloud arbitrarily decides

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:21.033
to move to the next town over.

00:16:22.765 --> 00:16:26.100
And yeah, they had built
this theory, which was...

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:26.933
You could...

00:16:26.933 --> 00:16:27.810
If you do it with confounders,

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:29.610
you could make it be predictive.

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:30.870
They put the Pope...

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:33.180
They surrounded the Pope with
fire and that protected him

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:35.850
but it was clear because it
was keeping the sick people out

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:37.400
and not any weird cloud groups.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:42.510
Yeah, so you could also
build a theory like that

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:44.460
but you would have to
constantly keep modifying it

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:48.480
based on weird confounders
and weird patterns that arise

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:51.510
because you have to
captured the real things.

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:53.851
And that is another thing that rescues us,

00:16:53.851 --> 00:16:55.782
to do biology, I don't need to simulate

00:16:55.782 --> 00:16:58.590
the standard model of particle physics

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:00.963
like you have suspected.

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:04.560
And indeed so in biology,

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.380
most of the time we end up using...

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:09.900
Most of our problems are combinatorial.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:11.460
They have to do about molecules

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:14.490
and concentrations of the
molecules in solutions.

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:18.390
But we don't need to
simulate atomic dynamics,

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:21.210
sometimes molecular but most of the time

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:22.680
it's combinatory problems and for those

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:24.603
you can reliably use simulations.

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:28.410
For instance, in this other work on

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:31.860
the package EmptyDrops, we had the...

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:33.900
There is a following
very important problem

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:35.163
in computational biology.

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:40.320
So right now in biology we can figure out,

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:41.490
like I was telling you,

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:43.560
different cells opt for different choices,

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:45.160
combination of genes to express.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:49.230
So, it's important to be able to sequence

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:52.860
and figure out what genes
each cell expresses.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:55.140
And in order to do that,
you have to capture the cell

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:58.260
and count all the molecules in it.

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:01.260
But sometimes you're trying
to capture it and you miss

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:03.753
and you capture next to it solution,

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:09.810
like yeah water with some
RNA and DNA molecules

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:12.210
in that random water that you...

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:14.850
Because those molecules leaked from cells

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:17.040
or nuclei into the water.

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:18.810
So sometimes you capture the cell

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:21.240
and sometimes you don't
but you think you have

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:23.700
because you still capture the RNA

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:26.250
and that risks confounding
downstream analysis

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:27.450
because sometimes you have noise

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:29.550
and you can mistake it to be a real cell,

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:32.760
and then start imagining
that you have a new cell type

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:35.373
so it's a huge problem
in computational biology.

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:41.190
And there are some
methods to deal with that.

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:43.020
For instance, there is
this famous algorithm

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:44.820
that we use in computational biology,

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.917
it's called CellRanger and it says that,

00:18:47.917 --> 00:18:49.833
"Oh come on, if you capture noise,

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:53.610
it must be so much less than a real cell

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:56.340
that has a lot of RNA and DNA in it."

00:18:56.340 --> 00:19:00.060
So I'm just gonna say
that all the observations

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:02.040
that have a lot of RNA
and DNA are real cells,

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.230
all the other ones are just noise.

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:08.640
But of course if the signal to
noise ratio starts being bad,

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:10.532
you cannot make the distinction.

00:19:10.532 --> 00:19:13.650
And yeah, so what if you have a cell

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:16.500
that has as much RNA as the noise,

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:18.330
then clearly you are either gonna have

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:19.890
too many false positives
or false negatives.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:22.383
You need a better method to do it.

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:27.480
And our idea was to go in the observations

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:32.010
that have very few RNA and DNA molecules.

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:36.600
So if it has 10 RNA
molecules that must be noise,

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:39.570
there's no cell with
just 10 RNA molecules.

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:42.090
So great, so those are surly noise.

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:43.740
And then if you put all of this together,

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:45.760
you can create a profile of the noise

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:48.000
and then you can go in the cases

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:50.130
where indeed they are ambiguous.

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:53.790
And so here's how many
DNA molecules you have

00:19:53.790 --> 00:19:55.380
and here how many RNA molecules you have

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:58.470
so in these ambiguous ones
that are in the middle

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.650
and you don't know, which they are,

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:02.880
since you have profiled the noise,

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:04.770
you can do a statistical hypothesis test

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:07.590
whether the observation
is statistically close

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:10.413
to the distribution you
created for the noise.

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:13.500
And if they deviate, you
think of them as cells

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:16.440
that was our idea, which doesn't make

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:18.890
personality assumptions
about the expected cells.

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:21.840
And indeed in the simulation

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:25.740
we show that we're doing a
lot better than CellRanger

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:28.110
because so, what we did in the simulation

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:30.870
is we took a data set that
processed by CellRanger,

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:32.970
CellRanger thinks that
the red ones are cells

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:36.390
and the black ones are not cell.

00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:38.910
And we took a thousand observations

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:40.800
of the ones it thinks our cells

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:44.490
and we downs sampled the
number of counts in each one.

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:47.220
So to simulate a small cell,
which should be realistic

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:51.750
because it's like a reductio
ad absurdum again, right?

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:54.810
So I'm saying that, "Oh okay,
competitor, I agree with you

00:20:54.810 --> 00:20:58.620
and you are right then
let's down sample that."

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:01.230
Then you can see here
that CellRanger does miss

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:03.480
the green population of small
cells, whereas we find it

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:07.230
because those, that
small cell still has...

00:21:07.230 --> 00:21:09.330
Is statistically different than the noise.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:13.800
Yeah...

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:14.810
So...

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:23.100
Yeah, excellent and in other projects

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:25.320
in the Dachman Lab, we're focusing

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:30.320
on the following very
important problem, which is,

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:35.070
so in multicellular organisms
in order to function

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:36.960
and since by definition
they have many cells,

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:38.250
those many different cells

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:40.443
need to coordinate their activities.

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:43.950
For instance, in my eyes see something,

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:45.720
the rest of my body
needs to potentially go

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:47.370
in a fight or flight mode,

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:50.170
so they need to communicate
the signal that my eyes got.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:53.130
So, there's this need of
cellular communication

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:54.750
and how they communicate...

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:56.460
At least one of the ways
they can communicate

00:21:56.460 --> 00:22:00.360
is by the sending cell
excreting a molecule

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:04.020
that we call the ligand,
that ligand moving on

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.090
until it reaches the receiving cell

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:08.550
that has another molecule on its surface

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:11.190
that we call the receptor
and it activates the receptor

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:13.540
and causes something
inside the receiving cell.

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:17.157
However, some receptors are orphan

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.650
in the sense that we don't
know any endogenous...

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:25.050
Any ligands that bind them

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:28.470
yet they're at very conspicuous
places, for instance,

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.390
in the part of the
heart that makes it pump

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:33.270
or in the brain and
conserved across species.

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:35.070
So they're not useless,
they're being used,

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:37.560
we just don't know how.

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:40.931
And that for one thing makes us

00:22:40.931 --> 00:22:43.320
not know enough fundamental biology

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.540
for another thing creates
potential side effects

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:47.280
that we don't understand for drugs

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:50.523
because they potentially
activate those receptors.

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:56.460
And yeah, also like in designing new drugs

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:58.950
that would potentially want
to activate those receptors,

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:02.061
would be important to find
which ligands activate them.

00:23:02.061 --> 00:23:06.360
And of course this has been
done, has been attempted

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:08.910
with protein structure
looking at the molecules

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:10.440
and trying to see how they fit together.

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:15.440
But our idea is to use
spatial genomic data sets,

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:18.660
for instance, what I showed there.

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:22.770
And since we now know at
each spot in this section,

00:23:22.770 --> 00:23:26.130
in this image, what genes
are expressed at this spot,

00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:29.880
we developed a hypothesis test

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:32.190
and we see that ligands are receptors

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:33.783
tend to be co-expressed.

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:37.600
So, the ligand is expressed

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:40.920
where there is a receptor to hear it.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.890
So nature in sense is not
wasteful in communicating.

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:45.750
It doesn't express a ligand

00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:48.120
in a location where it'll be useless

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:50.370
because there wouldn't be
any receptor to hear it.

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:52.890
Kind of like a tree falling in a forest

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:55.493
where there's nobody to hear
it, that doesn't happen.

00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:59.730
Yeah, they are called called co-localized,

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:00.630
they're nearby neighbors.

00:24:00.630 --> 00:24:02.970
So right now we're
developing a generating model

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:07.265
for trying to find gene programs

00:24:07.265 --> 00:24:11.793
shared between between cells.

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:18.150
Yeah so, and in doing so they'll be able

00:24:18.150 --> 00:24:21.390
to capture those ligand receptor pairs.

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:24.273
So gene programs define
the identity of a cell,

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:29.040
like I was saying, there's an eye program

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:32.463
that makes those eyes
in my eye, eye cells.

00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:35.790
But there's also those shared gene program

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:38.310
that capture those
interactions between cells

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:40.683
and that's what we're doing right now.

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:46.620
And yeah, that's scores and another point

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:49.893
and now we're tied with nature.

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:55.680
And yeah, so in summary, this
went by faster than I thought,

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:58.530
I don't know why but yeah,

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:00.663
in summary like EmptyDropsMultiome,

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:03.840
we developed this package

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:06.690
for powerful and accurate cell calling.

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:09.330
This needs to be for casual
model of covariate effects

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:12.210
on genomic information
and cell communication

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:15.300
for energy for de-orphanizing receptors.

00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:18.000
And yeah, I would like
to thank the labs in,

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:19.440
and we are such a great team.

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:21.570
Of course, I'm sure
we'll score another point

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:24.090
and put humanity ahead.

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:26.343
So yeah, thanks so much.

00:25:27.265 --> 00:25:30.265
(audience applauds)

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:34.800
- Thanks, Stathis that's
very, very interesting.

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:37.650
So I'm gonna take the prerogative

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:38.760
and ask the first question.

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:42.810
So, one is a comment and then a question.

00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:45.270
I mean there are
fundamental laws in nature,

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:47.201
we just don't know all of them.

00:25:47.201 --> 00:25:48.034
- Right, right, right.

00:25:48.034 --> 00:25:48.867
- I mean it's not...

00:25:48.867 --> 00:25:49.700
It's an incomplete set.

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:50.533
- Yeah.
- So, okay.

00:25:51.427 --> 00:25:53.310
And then I just wanted to clarify, right?

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:56.040
So the thinking on the information paradox

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:58.050
has evolved quite a lot.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:01.620
So I think Hawking himself
kind of changed his mind

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:03.480
as, you know, towards the end, right?

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:06.810
So he had a very nice analogy,
which I repeat quite often,

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.240
but I think it's instructive

00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:11.580
is that suppose you have
the Encyclopedia Britannica

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:14.370
and you look up New Haven and you know,

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:16.170
a hundred thousand people
population, whatever.

00:26:16.170 --> 00:26:19.050
Then you put that encyclopedia in a box,

00:26:19.050 --> 00:26:20.700
so it's printed, we read it,

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:22.320
